    <article class="section prose">
      <h1>Keepsafe alternative without an account (Android)</h1>
      <p>
        Keepsafe is one of the best-known Android photo vaults — Google Play lists it at
        <strong>50M+</strong> downloads with ads and in-app purchases on the free tier
        (<a href="https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.kii.safe">Play listing</a>,
        checked Nov 2026). Many people who search for a “Keepsafe alternative” are not asking for
        another cloud gallery. They want a vault that works <strong>without creating an email
        account</strong>, without ads, and with encryption details they can read.
      </p>
      <p>
        This page stays factual. It cites Keepsafe’s own help center and Play listing, notes what a
        third-party comparison observed about ads, and explains where Vireault fits — including what
        Vireault does <em>not</em> ship yet. It is not a claim that Keepsafe is unsafe for every
        user.
      </p>

      <h2>What Keepsafe’s public docs say about accounts and recovery</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Keepsafe’s help article
          <a href="https://support.getkeepsafe.com/hc/en-us/articles/360035454311-How-does-Keepsafe-protect-my-data"
            >How does Keepsafe protect my data?</a
          >
          describes 256-bit AES encryption at rest, Private Cloud backups, and PIN/pattern unlock.
          For a forgotten PIN it points to a reset process that uses an
          <strong>access code through your account email</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>
          The same article states that Basic (free) backs up the <strong>first 200 items</strong>;
          Premium raises the cloud cap (Keepsafe documents up to 10,000). It also warns that losing a
          device without backup means photos and videos are gone forever.
        </li>
        <li>
          A separate help article states Keepsafe is
          <strong>not currently designed as a completely zero-knowledge app</strong>, and lists
          limited cases where the company can view or share personal data (user-requested support,
          legal requirements, Terms/Privacy concerns):
          <a
            href="https://support.getkeepsafe.com/hc/en-us/articles/360035082512-Can-Keepsafe-see-any-of-my-content"
            >Can Keepsafe see any of my content?</a
          >.
        </li>
        <li>
          Trash recovery is documented as a <strong>Premium</strong> feature:
          <a href="https://support.getkeepsafe.com/hc/en-us/articles/360035445111-Recovering-items-from-Trash"
            >Recovering items from Trash</a
          >.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        None of that means Keepsafe “does not encrypt.” It means the product model includes an
        account, a vendor cloud with free-tier caps, and a trust boundary that is not zero-knowledge
        by Keepsafe’s own wording. If that is what you want to avoid, look for a local vault.
      </p>

      <h2>What “no account” actually buys you</h2>
      <p>A no-account Android vault typically means:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>No email or phone number required to open or unlock the vault</li>
        <li>No vendor login that can reset access to your ciphertext</li>
        <li>Keys derived on-device from a secret only you hold</li>
        <li>Backups as files <em>you</em> copy (USB, Files, your own Drive), not an automatic vendor cloud</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The tradeoff is blunt: if you forget the secret and lose the backup recovery material,
        <strong>nobody can restore the files</strong> — including the developer. That is the cost of
        not giving a company a recovery path. Read that before migrating irreplaceable media.
      </p>

      <Cta
        pageSlug={pageSlug}
        heading="Try a local vault with no signup"
        body="Vireault runs offline on Android: Argon2id + AES-256-GCM, no account, no ads. Free: 1 vault / 50 items. Pro from $1.99/week, $19.99/year (3-day trial), or $29.99 lifetime."
      />

      <h2>Checklist: Keepsafe alternative without an account</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Signup.</strong> Does the app insist on an email before you can store files?
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Crypto detail.</strong> Does it name a KDF and AEAD (for example Argon2id +
          AES-256-GCM), or only “military-grade”? Keepsafe’s Play listing uses the latter phrasing
          without a cipher mode on the store page.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Ads.</strong> York Vault’s Oct 2026 Android comparison of seven mainstream vaults
          (Keepsafe, NQ Vault, Hide It Pro, Calculator vault, Vaulty, LockMyPix, Private Photo Vault)
          reported that <strong>all seven</strong> Play listings said “Contains ads” on the free
          version (
          <a href="https://yorkvault.com/guides/best-photo-vault-apps-android"
            >yorkvault.com/guides/best-photo-vault-apps-android</a
          >). If you want a vault free of ad networks, filter for that explicitly.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Backup you control.</strong> Prefer an encrypted export you can restore after
          uninstall — and practice one restore before deleting gallery originals. See
          <a href="/guides/photo-vault-lost-photos-android">photo vault lost my photos</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Paywalls in front of your files.</strong> Check whether Trash recovery, basic
          unlock or export of <em>your</em> items requires Premium. Prefer products that never hold
          your ciphertext hostage.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Honest missing features.</strong> A good alternative page admits what is not built
          yet instead of promising a full Keepsafe clone on day one.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Where Vireault fits</h2>
      <p>
        Vireault is an Android vault built around the no-account model:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>No account, offline / local storage, no ads and no analytics in the product we ship against</li>
        <li><a href="/security">Argon2id key derivation</a> and AES-256-GCM for vault contents, with an encrypted index</li>
        <li>Unlock with PIN, 3×3 pattern or passphrase; lockout after wrong attempts</li>
        <li><a href="/features/backup-restore">Encrypted backup file</a> you export and restore yourself</li>
        <li>
          Free: 1 vault / 50 items. Pro: $1.99/week, $19.99/year with a 3-day trial, or
          <a href="/pricing">$29.99 lifetime</a> via Google Play
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        <strong>Not shipped yet</strong> (so this is not a feature-for-feature Keepsafe replacement):
        cloud sync across devices, biometrics, decoy / fake PIN vault, in-app camera. Folders are
        coming soon. There is no third-party audit claim on this page. Full limits:
        <a href="/security">/security</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Who might still prefer Keepsafe</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>You want vendor Private Cloud sync and are fine creating an account</li>
        <li>You already rely on Keepsafe’s workflow, Safe Send or multi-device habits</li>
        <li>You need mature Premium extras (album locks, break-in alerts, fake PIN) that Vireault has not shipped</li>
        <li>You value a long public review history over a newer indie vault</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Side-by-side category comparison:
        <a href="/compare/keepsafe">Vireault vs Keepsafe</a>. Keep both privacy policies open before
        you migrate private media.
      </p>

      <h2>Price note (lifetime seekers)</h2>
      <p>
        If your search is really “cheaper lifetime than LockMyPix,” that is a different intent.
        LockMyPix sells a separate
        <a href="https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.fourchars.lmp"
          >PREMIUM app</a
        >
        listed around <strong>$149.99</strong> one-time in the US on aggregators and Play mirrors
        (prices vary by country and change — check Play live). Vireault’s lifetime Pro is
        <strong>$29.99</strong>. That is a price comparison only; LockMyPix and Vireault differ in
        features, maturity and threat model. Prefer the product whose backup and crypto story you
        verified yourself.
      </p>

